    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Historical Building.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend multi-step instructions for academic tasks. Interactive: Respond to questions and requests in a school setting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of descriptions inspired by local artists or related to their work to complete an arts and crafts project (e.g., "Prepare materials for a painting inspired by Olga Sinclair's colorful murals"). Can respond to questions and requests related to arts and crafts in the school setting (e.g., "Who is your favorite artist?" What are some of their works?). Can summarize a peer's description of an artwork (e.g., "It is a painting that depicts Panamanian rural life").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage aims to engage students, activate prior knowledge related to the theme "This Is a Historical Building." and "Arts and Crafts," and introduce core vocabulary through clear modeling and pronunciation practice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images featuring famous historical buildings in Panama (e.g., Panamá Viejo, Casco Antiguo) and various Panamanian artworks or crafts (e.g., a mola, a tagua carving, a mural on a building, a sculpture). Students listen as the teacher briefly describes each image, using target vocabulary such as "building," "mural," "mola," "tagua carving," "artist," and "craft." Students point to the image being described.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "sculpture" (/ˈskʌlp.tʃər/), "design" (/dɪˈzaɪn/), "artist," "mural," and "colorful," emphasizing syllable stress. The teacher also models how to listen for descriptive adjectives and key nouns by saying a sentence like, "This is a <mark>colorful mural</mark> on a historical <mark>building</mark>," and then asking students to repeat the bolded words. The teacher demonstrates active listening by making eye contact and nodding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary and concepts.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a 'mural' a small painting on a canvas or a large painting on a wall?" (Expected answer: "A large painting on a wall.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does an 'artist' create beautiful things or break things?" (Expected answer: "Create beautiful things.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage provides authentic listening input related to the scenario "Arts and Crafts" and the theme "This Is a Historical Building.", introducing target grammar and vocabulary in context. Comprehension is checked through receptive tasks.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short descriptive narration (approx. 1-1.5 minutes). The recording describes a historical building (e.g., the Panama Canal Administration Building) and mentions a local artist who has created an artwork inspired by it (e.g., a painting on a canvas or a sculpture). The narration includes examples of Present Perfect ("This artist has created..."), Present Simple ("It is a..."), Past Simple ("Last year, she displayed..."), and introduces new vocabulary like "gallery," "exhibition," "canvas," "bold," "vibrant."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing creations ("The building is a two-story structure, with a rectangular design and white stone facades."), describing achievements of artists ("This artist has created beautiful sculptures."), and elements of sociolinguistic competence by understanding descriptions of art projects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> Students listen to the audio once. The teacher asks, "What is the main topic of this recording: a) how to cook Panamanian food, b) a historical building and an artist's work, or c) a new movie?" (Students raise hands for 'b').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Detailed Listening):</b> Students listen to the audio a second time. They are given a handout with T/F statements:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The building is a two-story structure." (T)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The artist has created abstract sculptures." (F - assuming the audio mentions colorful paintings)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The artist's work is currently in a gallery." (T/F depending on audio content)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen a third time. They are given two columns: Column A (Building features: e.g., "design," "facades") and Column B (Descriptions: e.g., "rectangular," "white stone"). Students match the building features to their descriptions as they hear them.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on developing accuracy in listening skills, specifically recognizing target grammar structures and vocabulary within different contexts through controlled and semi-controlled activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific grammar (Present Perfect, Present/Past Simple, Imperatives) and vocabulary (artist, craft, basket, sculpture, gallery, studio, exhibition, canvas, building, mural, mola, tagua carving; create, paint, sculpt, design, carve, display; creative, artistic, handmade, colorful, abstract, bold, vibrant, minimalist, timeless, unique, detailed). Pronunciation focus: identifying inflectional endings (-ed, -ing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Fill-in-the-Blanks (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a short audio segment (e.g., a description of another artwork or a simple instruction) with gaps in a transcript. Students listen carefully and fill in the missing words. These gaps specifically target the introduced vocabulary (e.g., "The artist has ______ a vibrant painting on a ______.") and verb forms, including inflectional endings (-ed, -ing). For example, "They are <mark>paint</mark>____ an old house," requiring students to listen for the -ing sound.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listen and Identify (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher reads aloud sentences. After each sentence, students raise their hands if they hear a specific grammar point or vocabulary. For example:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "This artist <b>has created</b> many beautiful sculptures." Students raise hands for "Present Perfect."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "Yesterday, we visited a small art <b>gallery</b>." Students raise hands for "Vocabulary word (gallery)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "First, you <b>prepare</b> the materials for the craft." Students raise hands for "Imperative."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher: "The artisan is <b>carving</b> a tagua seed." Students raise hands if they hear an "-ing" ending.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Listening for Instructions (Pair Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a set of mixed-up instruction cards for a simple craft (e.g., "making a paper mola"). The teacher reads the multi-step instructions aloud (e.g., "First, draw geometric shapes. Then, cut out the fabric. Finally, glue the pieces."). Students listen and arrange their cards in the correct sequence. They then check their sequence with their partner, briefly discussing any differences based on what they heard.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">13 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In this stage, students apply their listening comprehension in a realistic, interactive task, focusing on understanding and responding to descriptions related to "Arts and Crafts" and "Historical Buildings."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Listen and Draw (Individual Production)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher describes a simple "arts and crafts" project inspired by a historical building, using the target vocabulary (e.g., "building," "mural," "colorful," "geometric," "design," "create") and imperatives (e.g., "First, draw a large rectangle. Then, add a small, colorful door. Paint the roof vibrant red."). Students listen carefully to the multi-step instructions and draw the described artwork on a piece of paper. The teacher repeats the instructions at a slightly slower pace for a second listening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The drawing directly relates to creating an artwork inspired by the visual elements of a historical building, aligning with the "Arts and Crafts" scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency of comprehension, active listening, and following multi-step instructions accurately. The pragmatic function of "describing how to create something" is the core input for students' receptive listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This stage focuses on formative assessment to gauge students' comprehension of the listening input and provides opportunities for self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Peer Assessment</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Listen and Draw" artwork to a peer. The teacher circulates with an observation checklist, noting how accurately students followed the multi-step instructions and whether key descriptive elements (e.g., "colorful," "rectangular design") from the listening task are present in their drawings. Students then do a quick peer assessment: they exchange drawings with a partner and silently compare their drawings to their partner's, giving a thumbs up/down based on how well they think their partner understood the instructions. They don't provide verbal feedback at this stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Comprehension of multi-step instructions (evidenced by accuracy of drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of key descriptive elements and target vocabulary (e.g., "mural," "building," "colorful," "bold design").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall clarity of the resulting artwork as a representation of the listened description.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three new art-related words you understood today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to follow the drawing instructions? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This final stage allows students and the teacher to reflect on the learning process, identify areas of strength and areas for improvement, and bridge the lesson to the next in the sequence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher facilitates a brief class discussion or has students quickly jot down answers to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of listening to descriptions or instructions was most challenging for you today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one strategy you can use to improve your listening skills next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in terms of engaging students and facilitating listening comprehension? (e.g., Visuals, repetition of audio, clear modeling of pronunciation and syllable stress.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with multi-step instructions or specific vocabulary? (e.g., Provide more visual cues, slower pace, pre-teach more vocabulary with pictures, use simpler sentence structures.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effectively did the lesson introduce the core vocabulary for the theme?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Today, we focused on listening to understand descriptions of historical buildings and art. You've now heard many of our new vocabulary words. In Lesson 2, we will use these same words to practice our reading comprehension, seeing how they are used in written texts about arts and crafts."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">4 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>